<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The board: what the widget becomes once it has been given real room.

  ⚠️ **This is a SECOND layout, not a replacement.** `widget_lock.xml` and its root id are identity
  — renaming either orphans every instance already placed on a home screen — so it keeps serving
  the four small sizes exactly as it does today, and the two new tall breakpoints draw this
  instead. A fault in the board therefore cannot break the sizes people already have placed.

  ⚠️ **Named regions here, generic slots there, and the difference is deliberate.** `widget_lock.xml`
  records why its rows must be anonymous: it renders a RANKED LIST whose length and order change
  with the data, so named rows would freeze the running order at every size. A dashboard is the
  other kind of thing. Its regions are a designed arrangement — a header is never a cell value and
  they never trade places — so naming them lets the XML hold the design instead of the Kotlin
  rebuilding it on every render.

  Every region defaults to GONE and is switched on only when it has something to say, so a feed
  that failed costs its own block and leaves no gap. No `addView` anywhere: the whole board is
  pre-declared and the render is `setViewVisibility` + `setTextViewText`, which keeps the parcel to
  a few kilobytes of actions rather than nested RemoteViews.

  No background plate — like the small widget, this sits on the user's own wallpaper, so every line
  carries the drop shadow from `WidgetLine`.
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/widget_board_root"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="vertical"
